<exercise><exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <div class="os-raise-ib-input-ack">
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>To find the maximum height, find the vertex. Write the function in vertex form.</para>
    <para>\(H(t)=-16t^2+80t+40\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Factor -16 out of the first two terms.<br/>
    \(H(t)=-16(t^2+5t)+40 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 </emphasis>- Complete the square.<br/>
      \((\frac{5}{2})^2=\frac{25}{4}\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Add the constant to complete the square and subtract from the 40 to balance the equation. Note that because there is a \((-16)\) outside of the parentheses. This makes the constant equation to be \((-16 \times \frac{25}{4}=-100\).  Because a \((-100 )\) was really added in,  then a positive 100 will balance the equation.<br/>
      \(H(t)=-16(t^2+5t+\frac{25}{4})+40+100 \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor and simplify.<br/>
      \(H(t)=-16(t+\frac{5}{2})^2+140\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Find the vertex.<br/>
      \((-\frac{5}{2}, 140)\)</para>
  </div>
<problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    <para>A ball is thrown upward from the top of a 40 foot high building at a speed of 80 feet per second. The ball’s height above ground can be modeled by the equation \(H(t)=-16t^2+80t+40\). What is the maximum height?</para>
  </problem><solution>
